---
title: Modifications of Stochastic Processes with Continuous Sample Paths are Indistinguishable
date: 2026-04-27
---

Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are two stochastic processes indexed by an interval $\mathcal I \subset \mathbb R_+$, with continuous sample paths. If $X$ is a modification of $Y$ then the two processes are indistinguishable. For each $t \in \mathcal I$ there exists a null set $N_t$ such that $X_t(\omega) = Y_t(\omega)$ for all $\omega \notin N_t$. We construct
\[
    N_0 = \bigcup_{t \in \mathcal I \cap \mathbb Q} N_t,
\]
which is a null set as the countable union of null sets. We obtain $\Omega_0 = \Omega \setminus N_0$ a set of full measure such that $X_t = Y_t$ for all $t \in \mathcal I \cap \mathbb Q$. Since $X$ and $Y$ have continuous sample paths indistinguishability follows, let $t \in \mathcal I$ by density there exists an approximating rational sequence $(q_n)_{n \in \mathbb N}$ in $\mathcal I$ 
\[
    X_t = \lim_{n \to \infty} X_{q_n} = \lim_{n \to \infty} Y_{q_n} = Y_t.
\]
